# Secrets for the Stripe TEST-mode deployment. Copy to `.test.vars`, which is
# gitignored (every `*.vars` is), fill it in, and push it with
#
#   node ../../scripts/push-worker-secrets.mjs .test.vars --env test
#
# `--env test` is not optional. Wrangler applies secrets to the DEFAULT
# environment unless told otherwise, and the default here is the Worker taking
# real money at api.drakar.mgcrea.io. A test-mode webhook secret pushed there
# refuses every genuine payment as an invalid signature, and nothing says why
# until a buyer writes in. The `test` environment is declared in wrangler.jsonc,
# but its database is not created yet, so the first rehearsal starts with:
#
#   pnpm exec wrangler d1 create drakar-licenses-test
#     (paste the database_id it prints into the `test` environment's
#     d1_databases entry in wrangler.jsonc)
#   pnpm exec wrangler d1 migrations apply drakar-licenses-test --env test --remote
#   (create the test-mode Payment Link, with `price_id` in its metadata, and
#   paste its price id into the `test` environment's EXPECTED_PRICE_ID in
#   wrangler.jsonc, then `pnpm types`: empty, the rehearsal fulfils every
#   app's test checkouts with real drk1 keys)
#   pnpm exec wrangler deploy --env test
#
# and only then the secret push above.
#
# The same three keys as `.prod.vars`, from the test-mode side of the Stripe
# dashboard. The licence signing key is the SAME value as production: a key
# minted through a test purchase has to verify against the public key compiled
# into the app, or the end-to-end test proves nothing.
#
# Which makes every key a test purchase mints a REAL key, and a test-mode
# Payment Link is a public URL anybody can complete with card 4242. So
# `make revocations` reads this environment's database too, once its
# database_id is in wrangler.jsonc, and revokes every licence in it. A test key
# works in the build you are testing and in no released build after the next
# `make revocations`.
LICENSE_SIGNING_KEY=
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
